      "text": "To determine the number of rows in the display, we need to understand the pattern of the cans. The display starts with 1 can in the top row, then the second row has 3 cans, the third row has 5 cans, and so on. This sequence is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1\\) is 1 and the common difference \\(d\\) is 2.\n\nThe number of cans in the \\(n\\)-th row can be expressed as:\n\\[ a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1 \\]\n\nThe total number of cans in the display is the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of this arithmetic sequence. The sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\cdot (a_1 + a_n) \\]\nSubstituting the values we have:\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\cdot (1 + (2n - 1)) = \\frac{n}{2} \\cdot 2n = n^2 \\]\n\nWe know the total number of cans is 100, so we set up the equation:\n\\[ n^2 = 100 \\]\nSolving for \\(n\\), we get:\n\\[ n = \\sqrt{100} = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the display contains \\(\\boxed{10}\\) rows.",
